Package, depgraph, dep_check, dep_expand, digraph: local rewrites of hot statements

Sub-series: M (leftovers) - patch 1 of 1; applies to 3.0.83 with only the earlier patches of this sub-series.

Leftovers, each a local rewrite of a hot statement, none depending on
another: Package gets a __bool__ that needs no Python frame (truth was
taken from __len__, always 4); the metadata wrapper constructor copies the
slots in a direct loop; digraph.add() looks each node up once;
dep_expand() tests the category without splitting the cp;
_pkg_use_enabled() returns at once when no USE change is recorded;
dep_check() and dep_zapdeps() test the noise level before formatting debug
messages.

These are the local trims of patch 0025 of the 25-patch ladder
(config-trims-and-leftovers), which is now patches 0022, 0025, 0026, 0027
and 0030. With the two memos (now 0022 and 0026) they were 2.8 s as a step
of the second order that was measured (chain-prelim2); none of the pieces
is clearly above the noise alone. Together, on top of patches 0001-0024:
sum of six states 95.9 -> 90.5 s (-5.5 s, -1.2 % of the stock sum 447.5).
Alone on stock 3.0.83: see README (sub-series M).

Origin: written by AI agents (Claude) in the emerge-fast job, 2026-11-17..20;
isolated from the full patch and measured on 2026-11-20..21; cut out of its
former patch on 2026-11-21 without changing a line of code. Not a
human-written change. Base: Portage 3.0.83. Test: `emerge -p -uND @world` on
six states of one system image, one resolve at a time; every run gave the
same merge list in the same order, the same number of backtracking attempts
and the same package count as unpatched 3.0.83.
